1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Welcome back. On any large team, the plugins that make you productive need to reach your teammates, too.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Or, everyone ends up with a slightly different setup.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This lesson covers how to scope an install, how to share plugin configuration through version control,

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
and how to audit what's running so the whole team stays aligned.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Let's break it down.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,000
When you install a plugin, you pick where it applies, and there are four scopes to choose from.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
User is the default. The plugin becomes available across all of your own projects, but only for you.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Project is the shared one. It gets written into the project's settings file, so every collaborator

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,000
on that repo gets the plugin.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Local is narrower, just you and just this one repository, which is handy for trying something

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,000
without affecting your other work.

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
And Managed is the locked-down scope.

13
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Administrators install these through managed settings, and you can't modify or remove them yourself.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:04,000
So the four scopes really answer one question, how widely should this plugin apply?

15
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Let's zero in on the project scope, because that's the one that makes team sharing work.

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:16,000
When you install a plugin at project scope, the plugin settings get written into the project's settings file.

17
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
That file lives in the repository, and it's committed to source control right alongside your code.

18
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
So here's the payoff. When a teammate pulls the latest changes, they don't just get your code updates,

19
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
they also get the same registered marketplaces and the same plugins already configured.

20
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Nobody has to follow a setup document or install things by hand. The environment travels with the repo.

21
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,000
That's how you make sure everyone on the team is working with the exact same capabilities,

22
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
which keeps behavior consistent across the whole project.

23
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
It helps to group these scopes into two families. On one side, you have the personal scopes, user and local.

24
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
These stay with you. They never touch source control, so your teammates see nothing.

25
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,000
And each person manages their own. On the other side are the shared scopes, project and managed.

26
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:09,000
These reach the whole team, but they get there in very different ways.

27
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Project travels through the repository, so sharing is opt-in and visible in your commits.

28
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Managed is set centrally by administrators and locked, so individuals can't change it.

29
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,000
When you're deciding where to install something, this is the first question. Is this just for me or for everyone?

30
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
So how does a project actually record all of this? It comes down to a few settings keys, and it's

31
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,000
worth knowing their names.

32
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
The first is enabled plugins. It records which plugins are turned on in a given scope,

33
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,000
so the right ones activate when the project loads. The second is extra known marketplaces.

34
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:51,000
This lists the marketplaces a project trusts beyond the official one,

35
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,000
which is how a teammate can reach the same non-official plugins you're using.

36
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,000
And the third is plugin configs, which holds the per-plugin configuration values,

37
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:06,000
keeping each plugin's options consistent for everyone who shares the file.

38
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:13,000
You rarely edit these by hand, but knowing they exist helps you understand what's actually being shared.

39
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Before you trust a project's plugin setup, especially one you just cloned, it's worth taking stock

40
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:20,000
of what's actually there.

41
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Start with the list command. It shows you every plugin installed and the state each one is in.

42
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,000
From there, filter by enabled or disabled, so you can see at a glance which plugins are actually

43
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
active versus just sitting there dormant.

44
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Then check each plugin's scope, because that tells you who it affects, just you or every collaborator.

45
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
And finally, and this is the important one, review what each plugin is actually permitted to do

46
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,000
before you rely on it.

47
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:56,000
A quick audit like this takes a minute and saves you from running code you never really looked at.

48
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:03,000
This is a good moment for a serious word about trust, because plugins are powerful in a way that cuts both ways.

49
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:08,000
When a plugin runs, it runs with your full privileges. That means a plugin can do anything you can

50
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:09,000
do on your machine,

51
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,000
which is exactly why a malicious or careless one is dangerous.

52
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
So the rule is simple. Install only from marketplaces and authors you actually trust,

53
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:27,000
and give a plugin the same scrutiny you'd give any third-party dependency you pull into a project.

54
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
For teams and organizations, there's another layer.

55
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Administrators can restrict which marketplaces are allowed at all using managed settings,

56
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,000
so the whole organization stays inside an approved set of sources.

57
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Cleaning up matters just as much as installing, and there's a subtlety here worth catching.

58
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,000
When a capability is no longer needed, you have a few ways to handle it.

59
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Disabling a plugin pauses it while keeping it installed, which is perfect when you might want it back.

60
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Uninstalling deletes the plugin and its files for good.

61
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:08,000
But here's the part people miss. If you remove a marketplace, that also uninstalls every plugin that came from it.

62
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
So removing a catalog isn't a harmless cleanup, it takes its plugins with it.

63
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Whichever change you make, run reload plugins afterward, so the running session reflects it right away,

64
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:25,000
and you don't end up with broken references pointing at something that's no longer there.

65
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Let's pull the team practices together into a simple checklist.

66
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
First, commit the config. Put the project's settings file into source control,

67
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
so your plugin setup ships right along with the code.

68
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
Second, document the marketplace. If your project relies on any marketplace beyond the official one,

69
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:49,000
make a note of it so teammates understand where the plugins are coming from and aren't surprised.

70
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Third, re-audit on pull.

71
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Every time you pull new changes, take a moment to review any new or updated plugins before you run them,

72
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
because remember, they execute with your privileges.

73
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:08,000
Three small habits and your whole team stays in sync on plugins without anyone getting a nasty surprise.

74
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Here's the bottom line.

75
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Plugins give a team shared capabilities, but only if you scope and share them deliberately.

76
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Personal scopes stay with you, while project scope travels through source control to everyone.

77
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Just remember, plugins run with your privileges, so trust and auditing matter.

78
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:33,000
That wraps up managing plugins across a team. I'll see you in the next one.

